From: Artem Bambalov <artembambalov1993@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] c2softavcdec: write with the stride the buffer actually has

Backport of the upstream fix, which AOSP carries in
media/codec2/components/avc/C2SoftAvcDec.cpp on main and did not land on
Q.

The decoder hands ih264d the plane pointers of a mapped gralloc buffer and
tells it, separately, what the row pitch is. The pitch it names is its own:

    mStride = ALIGN128(mWidth);
    setParams(mStride, IVD_DECODE_FRAME);

and it then asks the pool for a buffer that wide, assuming the allocation
will come back with rows exactly that far apart. On most devices it does --
1920 is already aligned to anything gralloc cares about -- and nobody
notices the assumption.

This board's gralloc rounds the stride to a power of two. Measured on the
device, with the allocator and the lock instrumented:

    allocated 1920x1080 format 0x32315659 usage 0x33 -> stride 2048
    lockYCbCr yStride=2048 cStride=1024 chromaStep=1
              y=0xb0755000 cb=0xb09f8000 cr=0xb0971000

The layout is correct YV12 for a 2048 pitch: the V plane begins at
y + 2048*1080 and the U plane 1024*540 after it, and both pointers land
exactly there. Nothing is wrong with what gralloc allocated.

But ih264d was told 1920, so every luma row is written 128 bytes short of
where it belongs and the error accumulates down the frame -- the picture
shears, and the last 138240 bytes of the plane are never written at all and
show through as the green of zeroed chroma. Video thumbnails in Gallery are
where this shows, because that is the path that decodes in software.

Upstream reads the pitch from the buffer instead of assuming it, and
re-tells the decoder when it differs. mStride then means the stride of the
buffer in hand rather than a guess about it, so ensureDecoderState compares
and fetches with ALIGN128(mWidth) directly.

C2SoftHevcDec and C2SoftMpeg2Dec have the same assumption and the same
upstream fix; they are left for their own patch, since neither can be
tested here yet.

The size check in setDecodeArgs has to move with it. It was written when
displayStride and the width the block was fetched with were the same
number, and reads

    if (outBuffer->width() < displayStride || ...)

-- a width in pixels against a row pitch in bytes. Once the pitch comes
from the buffer, the two part company by exactly the padding, and on this
board the check turns down the very buffer it was handed: 384 wide with a
512 pitch is reported as "Output buffer too small: provided (384x240)
required (512x240)", setDecodeArgs returns false, and the component ends
the work item with C2_CORRUPTED. MediaCodec passes that on as err 0xe and
the frame never arrives, which is what an empty video thumbnail in the
gallery is made of.

A block is big enough when it is at least as wide as the picture, so that
is what it is now compared against. Its pitch is its own business.

And the block is asked for at the width of the picture, not rounded up.
The rounding was there to make the buffer wide enough for a pitch the
decoder chose on its own; now that it is told the buffer's own pitch it
serves no purpose, and it does harm. A 176 wide video was given a 256
wide block, and 256 is the width that reached the other end: the picture
arrives laid out 256 to the row while only 176 of them were written, so
it runs out of content 99 rows down a 144 row frame and the rest shows
through as the green of an untouched plane. Every other clip on hand is
a multiple of 128 wide, which is why exactly one thumbnail was wrong.

One more hand moves that pitch. The first time a header comes back the
component names a width of its own, ALIGN128 of the picture, and that call
goes behind the back of the one above: the decoder writes with the rounded
width from then on while the buffer is still laid out to its own pitch. The
guard says nothing about it, because mStride is not what changed.

Measured on this board, with the decoder asked what it wrote with: a 176
wide clip was given a block 176 wide whose rows are 512 apart, was told 512,
and wrote at 256 -- ALIGN128(176) -- shearing the frame and leaving the tail
of the plane green. Every other clip came out right, 1280 written at 1536,
1920 at 2048, 2560 at 2560, and the reason is only the order the two calls
happen to fall in: at 1920 the component's own call comes first and the
pitch here is set after it, while at 176 it comes last and stands. The guard
cannot tell the difference, because at 176 the pitch it had already agreed
for the 320x240 block the decoder starts on -- 512 -- is the same number the
real block wants, so mStride never changes and the guard never fires again.

So the guard compares against the pitch the decoder was actually given
rather than the one this component last decided on. mDecoderStride records
every pitch handed over in setParams, the disagreement is seen on the very
next call, and the buffer's own pitch is put back before another frame is
written into it.

---
diff --git a/media/codec2/components/avc/C2SoftAvcDec.cpp b/media/codec2/components/avc/C2SoftAvcDec.cpp
index 2be51dd49..814944322 100644
--- a/media/codec2/components/avc/C2SoftAvcDec.cpp
+++ b/media/codec2/components/avc/C2SoftAvcDec.cpp
@@ -471,6 +471,8 @@ status_t C2SoftAvcDec::setParams(size_t stride, IVD_VIDEO_DECODE_MODE_T dec_mode
         return UNKNOWN_ERROR;
     }
 
+
+    mDecoderStride = stride;
     return OK;
 }
 
@@ -518,10 +520,30 @@ bool C2SoftAvcDec::setDecodeArgs(ivd_video_decode_ip_t *ps_decode_ip,
                                  size_t inSize,
                                  uint32_t tsMarker) {
     uint32_t displayStride = mStride;
+    if (outBuffer) {
+        C2PlanarLayout layout;
+        layout = outBuffer->layout();
+        displayStride = layout.planes[C2PlanarLayout::PLANE_Y].rowInc;
+    }
     uint32_t displayHeight = mHeight;
     size_t lumaSize = displayStride * displayHeight;
     size_t chromaSize = lumaSize >> 2;
 
+    /*
+     * Only when the pitch can hold a row of the picture. ihevcd checks
+     * exactly that and turns down anything narrower with
+     * IHEVCD_INVALID_DISP_STRD, and a refused pitch leaves the decoder
+     * writing rows the width it last agreed to while the buffer is laid
+     * out for another -- which arrives as a sheared frame with the tail
+     * of the plane never written, showing through green. A block too
+     * narrow for the picture is caught below and the frame is dropped
+     * rather than decoded into the wrong shape.
+     */
+    if (displayStride >= mWidth && mDecoderStride != displayStride) {
+        mStride = displayStride;
+        if (OK != setParams(mStride, IVD_DECODE_FRAME)) return false;
+    }
+
     ps_decode_ip->u4_size = sizeof(ivd_video_decode_ip_t);
     ps_decode_ip->e_cmd = IVD_CMD_VIDEO_DECODE;
     if (inBuffer) {
@@ -537,7 +559,7 @@ bool C2SoftAvcDec::setDecodeArgs(ivd_video_decode_ip_t *ps_decode_ip,
     ps_decode_ip->s_out_buffer.u4_min_out_buf_size[1] = chromaSize;
     ps_decode_ip->s_out_buffer.u4_min_out_buf_size[2] = chromaSize;
     if (outBuffer) {
-        if (outBuffer->width() < displayStride || outBuffer->height() < displayHeight) {
+        if (outBuffer->width() < mWidth || outBuffer->height() < displayHeight) {
             ALOGE("Output buffer too small: provided (%dx%d) required (%ux%u)",
                   outBuffer->width(), outBuffer->height(), displayStride, displayHeight);
             return false;
@@ -640,6 +662,7 @@ status_t C2SoftAvcDec::resetDecoder() {
         return UNKNOWN_ERROR;
     }
     mStride = 0;
+    mDecoderStride = 0;
     (void) setNumCores();
     mSignalledError = false;
     mHeaderDecoded = false;
@@ -755,24 +778,21 @@ c2_status_t C2SoftAvcDec::ensureDecoderState(const std::shared_ptr<C2BlockPool>
         ALOGE("not supposed to be here, invalid decoder context");
         return C2_CORRUPTED;
     }
-    if (mStride != ALIGN128(mWidth)) {
-        mStride = ALIGN128(mWidth);
-        if (OK != setParams(mStride, IVD_DECODE_FRAME)) return C2_CORRUPTED;
-    }
     if (mOutBlock &&
-            (mOutBlock->width() != mStride || mOutBlock->height() != mHeight)) {
+            (mOutBlock->width() != (int32_t)mWidth || mOutBlock->height() != mHeight)) {
         mOutBlock.reset();
     }
     if (!mOutBlock) {
         uint32_t format = HAL_PIXEL_FORMAT_YV12;
         C2MemoryUsage usage = { C2MemoryUsage::CPU_READ, C2MemoryUsage::CPU_WRITE };
-        c2_status_t err = pool->fetchGraphicBlock(mStride, mHeight, format, usage, &mOutBlock);
+        c2_status_t err =
+            pool->fetchGraphicBlock(mWidth, mHeight, format, usage, &mOutBlock);
         if (err != C2_OK) {
             ALOGE("fetchGraphicBlock for Output failed with status %d", err);
             return err;
         }
         ALOGV("provided (%dx%d) required (%dx%d)",
-              mOutBlock->width(), mOutBlock->height(), mStride, mHeight);
+              mOutBlock->width(), mOutBlock->height(), mWidth, mHeight);
     }
 
     return C2_OK;
@@ -908,6 +928,10 @@ void C2SoftAvcDec::process(
         if (0 < s_decode_op.u4_pic_wd && 0 < s_decode_op.u4_pic_ht) {
             if (mHeaderDecoded == false) {
                 mHeaderDecoded = true;
+                /* This names a width of its own and so undoes the pitch
+                 * setDecodeArgs agreed with the buffer. mDecoderStride keeps
+                 * the record, and the next call through setDecodeArgs puts
+                 * the buffer's own pitch back before the next frame lands. */
                 setParams(ALIGN128(s_decode_op.u4_pic_wd), IVD_DECODE_FRAME);
             }
             if (s_decode_op.u4_pic_wd != mWidth || s_decode_op.u4_pic_ht != mHeight) {
diff --git a/media/codec2/components/avc/C2SoftAvcDec.h b/media/codec2/components/avc/C2SoftAvcDec.h
index ed27493f3..cde63c838 100644
--- a/media/codec2/components/avc/C2SoftAvcDec.h
+++ b/media/codec2/components/avc/C2SoftAvcDec.h
@@ -162,6 +162,12 @@ private:
     uint32_t mWidth;
     uint32_t mHeight;
     uint32_t mStride;
+    /* The pitch the decoder was last told to write with, which is not the
+     * same thing as mStride. The component hands the decoder a width of its
+     * own the first time a header comes back, and without a record of that
+     * there is no way to notice that the pitch just negotiated has been
+     * taken back under us. */
+    uint32_t mDecoderStride;
     bool mSignalledOutputEos;
     bool mSignalledError;
     bool mHeaderDecoded;
